        en: `
      <p><strong>Turning Torso</strong> is Sweden's tallest building and one of the most recognizable skyscrapers in Northern Europe. Located in Malmö on the shores of the Öresund Strait, it was constructed between <strong>2001 and 2005</strong>. The tower was designed by the renowned Spanish architect and engineer Santiago Calatrava, who based the concept on one of his sculptures representing a twisting human body.</p>

      <p>Standing <strong>190 metres</strong> tall with 54 floors, the building is famous for its dramatic twist. The upper section rotates approximately <strong>90 degrees</strong> relative to the base, creating the illusion that the tower spirals upward. This innovative engineering solution made Turning Torso one of the world's most distinctive skyscrapers.</p>

      <p>The structure consists of nine five-storey segments, each rotated slightly from the one below. Inside are luxury apartments, office space and conference facilities. The building was designed with advanced engineering solutions to withstand strong coastal winds while incorporating modern standards of energy efficiency and sustainability.</p>

      <p>The construction of Turning Torso became the symbol of Malmö's transformation. The former industrial harbour district evolved into a modern waterfront neighbourhood featuring residential buildings, university facilities and environmentally friendly urban planning. The tower quickly became the visual landmark of this redevelopment.</p>

      <p>Today Turning Torso is regarded as one of Europe's most celebrated examples of contemporary architecture. Although primarily a residential building without a permanent public observation deck, its twisting silhouette has become the defining image of Malmö and regularly appears in lists of the world's most remarkable skyscrapers.</p>
    `,
